  m_Name: Horse
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  exhibitName: Armour for Man and Horse
  description: "It is possible that this armor made for both man and horse originally
    belonged to a \"garniture,\" an armor with multiple customized exchange elements
    that could convert the basic suit to various field and sporting uses. The total
    combined weight of both the man's and horse's armor is 114 pounds. The etched
    decoration of this armor is of a type that became fashionable in northern Italy
    during the late 1500s. It consists of ornamental bands of etched figures, animals,
    portrait busts, and armor trophies. In addition, a coat of arms is represented
    seven times in different places\u2014the center of the breastplate and blackplate,
    the front and back of each pauldron (shoulder defense), and the center of the
    peytral (horse's breastplate). The coat of arms is that of the Colonna family
    quartered with another, still unidentified family. It probably belonged to an
    unknown member of the V\xF6ls-Colonna family from the South Tyrol, now part of
    northern Italy."
  audio: {fileID: 8300000, guid: b7169c56284012944b62a10251bce28a, type: 3}
